I'm not sure which part you find unconvincing. It may very well be the
"once" part that makes you hate something happening once.



> I also feel like there's some opacity here, because events of you fightin=
g
> more than once are made up of events of you fighting once. That is, when
> you're in a fight club and fight more than once, you can just split them =
up
> into several events of fighting more than once, each of which you hate, s=
o
> naturally you should hate the collective. va'i you're sort of opening up
> the abstractor in a way that you really shouldn't be able to do in order =
to
> get the meaning you want.
>

"N roi" always needs an (implicit or explicit) interval in which the times
are counted. In my example, the implicit interval was each weekend. You can
always make it more precise, at the usual price of using more words. "I
hate it when you fight once (in a weekend)".

